Dislocations as line defects and carriers of slip (@mat-dislocations: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF", #source "../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z", #materials-engineering "Boundaries-stacking-faults-twins-and-surfaces.lingua#r_6CT9K8T9PC33CV70N0J375FA2D") { r_61JVKWJTPQB32RTQQH29D3PWTD
**Read:** [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), Introduction to Dislocations in full, including three-dimensional geometry, motion and observation; [Fundamentals of Materials Science — MIT 3.012](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Materials-Science-MIT-3-012.lingua#r_1JE4A3P3KKM0FZXERWFATXCS1Z), crystal foundations. Continue into later mechanical-property studies for quantitative strengthening.

A dislocation is a line defect characterized locally by its line direction and a Burgers vector under a declared circuit convention. Edge and screw descriptions refer to their relative orientation; a curved line can have mixed character. The Burgers vector is not generally the direction the line moves.

Moving a dislocation can change relative positions across a slip plane without shearing an entire perfect plane simultaneously. Glide and climb involve different geometry and transport requirements. Obstacles, temperature, stress and interactions affect motion, so the existence of a geometrically possible slip system does not determine its activity.

**Work:** Draw a consistent Burgers circuit for edge and screw examples, tracking the sign convention. Explain how line orientation can change while a conserved Burgers vector persists. Compare a direct structural observation with a mechanical inference and describe what evidence is required to connect them.

**Return:** [Materials engineering](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
